**Short answer:** Yes. You can keep Leaflet as the “app shell” and add a **MapLibre GL** map as a single Leaflet layer. The plugin **`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`** (formerly `maplibre-gl-leaflet`) does exactly that – it creates a Leaflet `L.GridLayer` that internally runs a MapLibre GL map, pulls vector-tile sources, and renders them on a canvas that Leaflet treats as a raster tile.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the “gotchas” you’ll run into most often.

---

## 1. When to use the two-source pattern

| Situation | Recommended approach |
|-----------|----------------------|
| You already have a Leaflet UI (controls, pop-ups, other raster overlays) and just need a modern, style-driven basemap | **Leaflet + MapLibre-GL-Leaflet** – one extra layer that renders a vector-tile style |
| You need full MapLibre interactivity (feature query, 3-D terrain, custom paint) on top of Leaflet | Same as above – the MapLibre canvas can be queried via the MapLibre API (`map.queryRenderedFeatures`) and you can forward the results to Leaflet UI |
| You only need a static raster basemap | Use a normal `L.TileLayer` with a raster tile URL (cheaper, no extra dependency) |

If you need **vector-tile basemap + your own data** you can keep your data in Leaflet (e.g. `L.geoJSON`) or add it as a second source inside the MapLibre style – both work because the MapLibre canvas is just another Leaflet layer.

---

## 2. Install the plugin (and MapLibre GL)

```bash
# npm / yarn (recommended for a build pipeline)
npm i maplibre-gl @maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet

# or, if you prefer CDN scripts:
#   <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
#   <script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@latest/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Why both packages?**  
> `maplibre-gl` provides the core WebGL renderer. `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet` is a thin wrapper that creates a Leaflet `GridLayer` and synchronises map movements, zoom levels, and CRS between Leaflet and MapLibre.

---

## 3. Basic Leaflet + MapLibre setup

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL basemap</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <style>#map { height:100vh; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@1.0.0/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise a plain Leaflet map (any CRS works, but most basemaps use EPSG:3857)
  const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
    center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
    zoom: 12,
    // optional: disable Leaflet's default tile layer so the background is transparent
    // (the MapLibre canvas will fill the view)
    preferCanvas: false
  });

  // 2️⃣ Create the MapLibre-GL-Leaflet layer
  const mapLibreLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    // a full MapLibre style JSON URL (or an inline style object)
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // public demo style
    // optional: pass any MapLibre GL options you would normally use
    // e.g. `attributionControl: false` if you want Leaflet to handle attributions
    attributionControl: false,
    // optional: set a maxZoom that matches the source tiles (helps Leaflet's tile cache)
    maxZoom: 22
  }).addTo(leafletMap);

  // 3️⃣ (Optional) Forward MapLibre events to Leaflet UI
  // Example: show a popup with feature properties on click
  mapLibreLayer.getMap().on('click', e => {
    const features = mapLibreLayer.getMap().queryRenderedFeatures(e.point);
    if (features.length) {
      const props = features[0].properties;
      L.popup()
        .setLatLng(e.lngLat)
        .setContent(`<pre>${JSON.stringify(props, null, 2)}</pre>`)
        .openOn(leafletMap);
    }
  });

  // 4️⃣ Add any Leaflet overlays you already have (markers, geoJSON, etc.)
  // They will be drawn *above* the MapLibre canvas by default.
  L.marker([40.7128, -74.0060]).addTo(leafletMap);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Leaflet side | MapLibre side | Interaction |
|------|--------------|---------------|-------------|
| **Map creation** | `L.map` creates the container, handles panning/zooming, UI controls, etc. | `L.maplibreGL` creates an internal `maplibre-gl` instance that shares the same view. | The plugin listens to Leaflet’s `move`, `zoom`, `resize` events and forwards them to the GL map, and vice-versa. |
| **Style** | You give a **style URL** (or an inline style object). The style can contain any number of sources – vector basemap, raster overlays, `raster-dem`, etc. | MapLibre loads the style, fetches its TileJSON, and renders tiles on a WebGL canvas. | The canvas is inserted into Leaflet’s tile pane (`L.GridLayer`), so Leaflet treats it like any other raster tile layer. |
| **Events** | Leaflet’s `click`, `mousemove`, etc. still fire on the map container. | MapLibre’s own `click` events are emitted on the internal GL map; you can retrieve them via `mapLibreLayer.getMap()`. | You can mix both – e.g., use Leaflet pop-ups for your own data and MapLibre queries for basemap features. |
| **Layer order** | Leaflet draws its own layers (markers, GeoJSON, etc.) on top of the MapLibre canvas by default. | If you need a MapLibre layer *above* a Leaflet overlay (rare), you can add a second `L.maplibreGL` layer with a higher `zIndex`. | Usually you keep the basemap as the GL layer and everything else as Leaflet overlays. |

---

## 4. Choosing the **style** (the vector-tile source)

| Option | Where to get it | Typical URL pattern | When to use |
|--------|----------------|---------------------|-------------|
| **Hosted public style** (MapLibre demo, OpenStreetMap, MapTiler, OpenFreeMap) | Free public endpoints | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json` | Prototyping, small-scale apps, demos |
| **Your own style URL** (self-hosted TileJSON + vector tiles) | Your server, Cloudflare Workers, AWS S3, PMTiles, etc. | `https://mytiles.example.com/style.json` | Production, custom basemap, branding |
| **Hybrid style** (vector basemap + raster-dem + raster imagery) | Mix of URLs inside one style JSON | See example below | When you need terrain, satellite imagery, or custom raster overlays |

**Tip:** If you already have a Mapbox-style URL (e.g. `https://api.mapbox.com/styles/v1/...`), you can **replace the token** with a MapLibre-compatible endpoint (MapTiler, MapLibre Cloud, or your own TileServer-GL). The style JSON format is identical.

---

## 5. Adding your own data **without** rebuilding the basemap tiles

The most common pattern is **“vector basemap + separate overlay source”**. You have two choices:

### 5.1 Keep the overlay in Leaflet (GeoJSON, markers, etc.)

```js
// Example: a GeoJSON of store locations
fetch('/data/stores.geojson')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(geojson => {
    L.geoJSON(geojson, {
      pointToLayer: (feat, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, { radius: 6, fillColor: '#ff6600' })
    }).addTo(leafletMap);
  });
```

*Pros*: No extra tile pipeline, easy to edit client-side, full interactivity.  
*Cons*: All features are downloaded at once (same GeoJSON limits as in the skill). Good for < 5 k features.

### 5.2 Add a **second MapLibre source** (vector tiles or GeoJSON) inside the same style

If you already have a style JSON, just add another source entry:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://mytiles.example.com/basemap.json"
    },
    "stores": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "https://myapp.example.com/data/stores.geojson"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    // basemap layers (roads, water, etc.) …
    {
      "id": "store-points",
      "type": "circle",
      "source": "stores",
      "paint": {
        "circle-radius": 6,
        "circle-color": "#ff6600"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*Pros*: The overlay is styled with the same MapLibre paint rules, can be queried with `map.queryRenderedFeatures`, and respects zoom-dependent styling.  
*Cons*: You need to host the style JSON (or embed it inline) and make sure CORS allows the GeoJSON fetch.

**How to use it with Leaflet:** The same `L.maplibreGL` call works; the style already contains both sources. No extra Leaflet code needed.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **CORS errors when loading the style or tiles** | The style JSON or TileJSON points to a domain that doesn’t send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` | Serve tiles with proper CORS headers (most static-file CDNs do this automatically). If you control the server, add `Header set Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"` (or a whitelist). |
| **MapLibre canvas appears blurry on high-DPI screens** | Leaflet’s default tile size is 256 px; the plugin automatically sets the GL canvas to the same size, but the device pixel ratio may be > 1. | The plugin respects `L.Browser.retina` and requests `@2x` tiles if the source provides them. Ensure your vector tile source includes `@2x` (or `scale` parameter) or set `pixelRatio: window.devicePixelRatio` in the `L.maplibreGL` options. |
| **Attribution missing or duplicated** | Leaflet’s default attribution control and MapLibre’s own attribution control can clash. | Disable one of them (`attributionControl: false` in the GL options) and let Leaflet handle all attributions via `map.attributionControl.addAttribution(...)`. |
| **Layer order not what you expect** | Leaflet draws its own layers *after* the GL canvas, but within the GL style the order of layers matters (e.g., you want your polygons under the basemap labels). | Insert your custom layers **before** the first symbol layer in the style. The skill “maplibre-source-wiring” shows a helper to find the first symbol layer and splice your layer in. |
| **Click events fire on the wrong layer** | Leaflet’s click handler may capture the event before the GL map sees it, or vice-versa. | Use `mapLibreLayer.getMap().on('click', …)` for features that belong to the vector tiles, and `leafletMap.on('click', …)` for Leaflet overlays. Both can coexist. |
| **Performance drop on mobile** | The GL canvas is heavy; combined with many Leaflet overlays it can exceed GPU limits. | Keep the number of Leaflet vector layers low (e.g., use a single `L.geoJSON` with `pointToLayer` rather than thousands of individual markers). Consider simplifying your basemap style (fewer layers, lower `source-layer` detail) for mobile. |
| **MapLibre terrain (`raster-dem`) doesn’t show** | Terrain requires a `terrain` property in the style and a `raster-dem` source. The plugin passes the style unchanged, but the underlying GL map must have `terrain.exaggeration` set. | Add to your style JSON: `"terrain": { "source": "dem", "exaggeration": 1.5 }` and a `raster-dem` source named `"dem"`. The plugin will render 3-D terrain automatically. |

---

## 7. Full-featured example (vector basemap + raster-dem terrain + GeoJSON overlay)

```js

**Short answer:** Yes.  Use the **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin, which wraps a MapLibre GL JS map as a Leaflet layer.  The plugin lets you point the MapLibre GL map at any vector-tile style (a MapLibre style JSON) and then treat that map exactly like any other Leaflet tile layer – you can pan/zoom together with your existing Leaflet overlays, add Leaflet markers, pop-ups, etc.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a minimal working example and a few gotchas.

---

## 1. What the plugin does (in a sentence)

`maplibre-gl-leaflet` creates a **Leaflet `Layer`** whose internal rendering engine is **MapLibre GL JS**.  
The MapLibre map receives the same style JSON you would use in a pure MapLibre app, so you get a full-featured vector-tile basemap (styling, interactivity, high-DPI rendering) while the rest of your app stays in Leaflet.

---

## 2. Install / load the libraries

| Method | What you need |
|--------|---------------|
| **npm / Yarn** (recommended for modern builds) | ```bash npm i maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet```<br>or ```yarn add maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet``` |
| **Plain `<script>` tags** (quick demo) | ```html <script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>```<br>```html <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>```<br>```html <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>```<br>*(versions may be newer – check the CDN for the latest)* |

> **Note:** `maplibre-gl-leaflet` depends on **MapLibre GL JS** (the rendering engine) **and** **Leaflet**.  Make sure both are loaded *before* the plugin script.

---

## 3. Create the Leaflet map

```javascript
// Basic Leaflet map (you can keep any existing Leaflet layers you already have)
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // example: New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: set maxZoom/minZoom to match the vector-tile source
  maxZoom: 18,
  minZoom: 0
});
```

If you already have a Leaflet map instance, just reuse it.

---

## 4. Add the MapLibre-GL vector basemap

```javascript
// 1️⃣  Choose a style URL that points to a MapLibre style JSON.
//    It can be a hosted style (e.g. from MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, your own server)
//    or a local file served with CORS enabled.
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_API_KEY';

// 2️⃣  Create the MapLibre-GL-Leaflet layer
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // The MapLibre style (vector tiles, glyphs, sprites, etc.)
  style: styleUrl,

  // Optional: pass any MapLibre GL options you would normally use.
  // For example, to enable terrain:
  //   terrain: { source: 'terrainSource', exaggeration: 1.5 }
  //   attributionControl: false   // Leaflet will handle attribution
}).addTo(map);

// 3️⃣  (Optional) Move the GL layer behind/above other Leaflet layers
//     Leaflet respects the order you add layers.  If you want your
//     own Leaflet markers on top, add them *after* the GL layer.
//     If you need the GL layer under a Leaflet raster overlay, add the
//     overlay later or use `glLayer.bringToBack()` / `bringToFront()`.
```

That’s it – the vector basemap now moves in lock-step with the rest of your Leaflet map.

### Minimal HTML demo

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL basemap</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <style>#map { height: 100vh; width: 100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  const map = L.map('map', {center: [51.505, -0.09], zoom: 13});

  const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_API_KEY'
  }).addTo(map);

  // Example Leaflet overlay – a marker that stays on top
  L.marker([51.5, -0.09]).addTo(map)
    .bindPopup('I am a Leaflet marker')
    .openPopup();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with a valid key from the tile provider (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.) or use a style that does **not** require a key.

---

## 5. Things to watch out for

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix / tip |
|-------|----------------|-----------|
| **CORS errors** when loading the style or tiles | The style JSON, vector-tile URLs, glyphs, or sprites must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or at least the origin of your page). | Host the style on a server you control, or use a provider that already sets CORS headers (MapTiler, OpenStreetMap-based services). |
| **Attribution duplication** | Both Leaflet and the MapLibre GL style may try to render attribution controls. | Turn off the MapLibre attribution (`attributionControl: false`) and let Leaflet’s built-in `L.control.attribution` handle it, or keep the GL control and hide Leaflet’s. |
| **Zoom limits mismatch** | Vector tiles often stop at `maxzoom: 14-16`. Leaflet’s default `maxZoom` is 18, which can cause empty tiles at higher zooms. | Set `maxZoom` on the Leaflet map to the highest zoom level the vector source provides, or enable `maxZoom` in the GL layer options (`maxZoom: 18`). |
| **High-DPI / Retina** | MapLibre GL automatically requests `@2x` tiles when `devicePixelRatio > 1`. Some tile providers only publish `1x` tiles. | If you see blurry basemap on retina screens, either switch to a provider that offers `2x` tiles or set `pixelRatio: 1` in the GL layer options (but you lose crispness). |
| **Performance on low-end devices** | Vector tiles are compact, but rendering many layers (labels, symbols) can be heavy on old phones. | Keep the style lean: disable unnecessary symbol layers, simplify geometry, or fall back to a raster basemap for those devices. |
| **Mixing Leaflet raster overlays with GL vector labels** | The GL layer draws *above* Leaflet raster layers by default. | Use `glLayer.bringToBack()` if you want the raster imagery on top, or add the raster layer *after* the GL layer (`L.tileLayer(...).addTo(map)`). |
| **MapLibre GL version mismatch** | The plugin is tied to a specific major version of MapLibre GL. | Use the same major version for both packages (`npm i maplibre-gl@4 maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13`). Check the plugin’s `peerDependencies` for the exact range. |

---

## 6. When you *might* prefer a pure-Leaflet solution

If you only need a **static raster basemap** (satellite imagery, a pre-rendered PNG/WEBP tileset) and you don’t care about client-side styling, you can skip MapLibre GL entirely and use a regular `L.tileLayer`.  

If you want **vector tiles but don’t need the full MapLibre style engine**, Leaflet’s own `L.vectorGrid` (from the `leaflet.vectorgrid` plugin) can render Mapbox Vector Tiles directly.  It’s lighter weight but lacks the full style spec support (e.g., text layout, complex filters).  

In most cases where you already have a **MapLibre style JSON** (or you want to use a hosted vector basemap like MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, or a custom style you built with TileServer-GL), the `maplibre-gl-leaflet` approach is the simplest and gives you the same visual fidelity as a pure MapLibre app.

---

## 7. Quick checklist before you ship

1. **Style URL reachable & CORS-enabled** – test in the browser console (`fetch(styleUrl)` should succeed).  
2. **Attribution** – ensure the required provider attribution appears (Leaflet’s control is the easiest place).  
3. **Zoom limits** – set `maxZoom`/`minZoom` on the Leaflet map to match the tile source.  
4. **Layer order** – add your Leaflet overlays *after* the GL layer if they should appear on top.  
5. **Mobile testing** – check performance on a low-end phone; consider a raster fallback if needed.  
6. **Version lock** – pin `maplibre-gl` and `maplibre-gl-leaflet` to compatible versions in `package.json`.  

---

## 8. TL;DR code snippet

```javascript
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import { MapLibreGL } from 'maplibre-gl-leaflet';

// 1️⃣ Leaflet map
const map = L.map('map', {center: [0,0], zoom: 2});

// 2️⃣ MapLibre GL layer (vector-tile basemap)
const glBasemap = new MapLibreGL({
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  attributionControl: false   // let Leaflet handle it
}).addTo(map);

// 3️⃣ Optional Leaflet overlay (marker, GeoJSON, etc.)
L.marker([0,0]).addTo(map).bindPopup('Hello from Leaflet!');

// 4️⃣ Add attribution (if you disabled the GL control)
L.control.attribution({prefix: false})
  .addAttribution('© <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a>')
  .addTo(map);
```

That’s all you need to blend a MapLibre-style vector tile basemap into a Leaflet application. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** Yes. You can keep Leaflet as the “app shell” and add a **MapLibre GL** map as a single Leaflet layer. The plugin **`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`** (formerly `maplibre-gl-leaflet`) does exactly that – it creates a Leaflet `L.GridLayer` that internally runs a MapLibre GL map, pulls vector-tile sources from a style URL (or a custom style object), and renders them on top of the Leaflet canvas.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the most common gotchas and a few optional enhancements.

---

## 1. Install the plugin (and MapLibre GL)

```bash
# npm / yarn
npm i maplibre-gl @maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet   # peer-dependency installs maplibre-gl automatically
# or, if you prefer a CDN:
#   <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
#   <script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@latest/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Why the separate `maplibre-gl` package?**  
> The Leaflet plugin does **not** bundle MapLibre GL; it expects the global `maplibregl` (or the ES-module import) to be present. This keeps bundle size low and lets you pin the GL version you need.

---

## 2. Create a normal Leaflet map

```js
// Basic Leaflet map (you can keep any existing Leaflet layers you already have)
const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060],
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: disable the default Leaflet tile layer if you only want the GL basemap
  // layers: [] 
});
```

---

## 3. Add the MapLibre-GL layer

```js
// 1️⃣  Choose a vector-tile style URL (or build your own style JSON)
//    Example: OpenStreetMap-based free style from MapTiler
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY';

// 2️⃣  Create the GL-Leaflet layer
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // Either a style URL…
  style: styleUrl,

  // …or an inline style object (useful if you want to inject your own sources)
  // style: {
  //   version: 8,
  //   sources: { ... },
  //   layers:  [ ... ],
  //   glyphs: 'https://.../{fontstack}/{range}.pbf',
  //   sprite: 'https://.../sprite'
  // },

  // Optional: pass MapLibre GL options (e.g., preserveDrawingBuffer for screenshots)
  // maplibreOptions: { preserveDrawingBuffer: true },

  // Optional: set the initial view (overrides Leaflet's view if you want them synced)
  // center: [40.7128, -74.0060],
  // zoom: 12,
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

**What happens under the hood**

| Leaflet side | MapLibre side | Interaction |
|--------------|---------------|-------------|
| `L.maplibreGL` creates a `L.GridLayer` that owns a hidden `<canvas>` | A full MapLibre GL `Map` instance is instantiated with the supplied style | The plugin forwards Leaflet’s `move`, `zoom`, `resize`, and `bearing` events to the GL map, so the two stay perfectly in sync. |
| The GL canvas is drawn into each Leaflet tile cell (the same size as a normal raster tile) | Vector tiles are fetched according to the style’s `sources` (usually `type: "vector"` with a TileJSON URL) | The GL map renders everything client-side (labels, icons, hillshade, etc.) just like a native MapLibre map. |

---

## 4. (Optional) Add your own Leaflet layers on top

Because the GL layer is just another Leaflet layer, you can stack additional Leaflet overlays **above** or **below** it:

```js
// Example: a Leaflet GeoJSON overlay (points, polygons, etc.)
const myGeojson = L.geoJSON(myData, {
  style: {color: '#ff7800', weight: 2},
  pointToLayer: (feature, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, {radius: 6, fillColor: '#ff0'})
}).addTo(leafletMap);   // <-- added *after* glLayer => drawn on top
```

If you need a **vector-tile overlay** (e.g., your own data as a vector tileset), you **don’t** add another `L.maplibreGL` instance. Instead, **extend the same style**:

```js
// 1️⃣  Load the style JSON, inject your own source, then re-create the GL layer
fetch(styleUrl)
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(style => {
    // Add a new source (vector tiles, GeoJSON, raster, etc.)
    style.sources.myData = {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'   // TileJSON URL or direct tile URL template
    };
    // Add a layer that draws from that source
    style.layers.push({
      id: 'my-data-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'myData',
      'source-layer': 'my_layer_name',   // name inside the vector tileset
      paint: {'fill-color': '#00ff00', 'fill-opacity': 0.4}
    });
    // Re-create the GL layer with the modified style
    const glLayer2 = L.maplibreGL({style}).addTo(leafletMap);
  });
```

> **Tip:** If you only have a **GeoJSON** file and you don’t want to run a tile server, you can also inject it as a `geojson` source in the style (the same way you would in a pure MapLibre map). The plugin will handle it automatically.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **CORS errors when fetching vector tiles** | Tile server does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) | Enable CORS on the tile server, or proxy the tiles through a same-origin endpoint. |
| **Missing glyphs / icons** | The style’s `glyphs` or `sprite` URLs are unreachable or not supplied | Provide explicit `glyphs` and `sprite` URLs in the style (or host them yourself). MapLibre GL ≥ 5.11 will fall back to a system font for text, but you’ll lose the intended look. |
| **MapLibre GL version mismatch** | The plugin was built against an older GL version, causing `maplibregl` errors | Use the latest versions of both `maplibre-gl` and `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`. They are kept in sync via peer-dependency. |
| **Layer order not as expected** | Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added; the GL layer is a single tile layer, so any Leaflet overlay added *after* it will appear on top. | Add Leaflet overlays **after** the GL layer if you want them on top, or **before** if you want them underneath. |
| **Performance on low-end mobile** | Vector-tile rendering is more CPU-intensive than raster tiles. | – Use a basemap style that limits the number of layers at low zooms (many providers already do this). <br>– Enable `maxZoom`/`minZoom` on the GL layer to avoid loading high-detail tiles when not needed. <br>– Consider a raster basemap for very constrained devices. |
| **Map rotation / pitch not working** | Leaflet’s default CRS (`EPSG:3857`) does not expose bearing/pitch controls. | The plugin forwards `rotate` and `pitch` only if you enable Leaflet’s `preferCanvas: true` and call `leafletMap.setBearing()` / `leafletMap.setPitch()` (available via the plugin’s `maplibreGL` instance). Most apps keep a flat 2-D view, so this is rarely needed. |

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL basemap</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <style>#map { height: 100vh; width: 100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@1.0.0/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Leaflet map
  const map = L.map('map', {
    center: [40.7128, -74.0060],
    zoom: 12,
    // optional: disable default Leaflet tile layer
    // layers: []
  });

  // 2️⃣ MapLibre GL basemap layer
  const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ Example Leaflet overlay (GeoJSON)
  fetch('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/glynnbird/usstatesgeojson/master/california.geojson')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(data => {
      L.geoJSON(data, {
        style: {color: '#ff6600', weight: 2, fillOpacity: 0.2}
      }).addTo(map);
    });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a free key from [MapTiler Cloud](https://cloud.maptiler.com/). Any other vector-tile style URL (MapLibre-compatible) works the same way.*

---

## 7. When you *don’t* need the plugin

| Situation | Simpler alternative |
|-----------|---------------------|
| You only need a **raster** basemap (satellite imagery, WMS, WMTS) | Use Leaflet’s native `L.tileLayer(urlTemplate, {...})`. |
| You already have a **MapLibre-only** app and just want a few Leaflet UI widgets | Keep MapLibre GL as the primary map and embed Leaflet controls via the `leaflet` package (the plugin works both ways). |
| You need **offline** maps on a kiosk with no network | Bundle a single `PMTiles` file and use the `maplibre-pmtiles-patterns` skill; you can still wrap it in the GL-Leaflet layer if you want Leaflet UI. |

---

## 8. TL;DR checklist

1. **Add the plugin** (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`) and `maplibre-gl` to your project.  
2. **Create your Leaflet map** as usual.  
3. **Instantiate `L.maplibreGL({style: …})`** with a MapLibre-compatible style URL (or inline style).  
4. **Add the layer to the Leaflet map** (`.addTo(map)`).  
5. **(Optional) Extend the same style** with your own sources/layers if you need vector-tile overlays.  
6. **Handle CORS, glyphs, and sprites** – make sure the style’s URLs are reachable from the browser.  
7. **Add any additional Leaflet overlays** (markers, GeoJSON, heatmaps) after the GL layer to draw them on top.  

That’s it – you now have a fully interactive Leaflet application that renders a **MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap** (with crisp labels, dynamic styling, and optional terrain) while still leveraging all of Leaflet’s UI ecosystem. Happy mapping!
